<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ciarán of Saighir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ciarán the Elder, Ciarán of Saigir, Saint Kieran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ciarán of Saigir, also called Ciarán the Elder, was an early Irish saint, traditionally regarded as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ossory and as active even before Saint Patrick. He founded a monastery at Saigir, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seir-kieran">Seir Kieran</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-offaly">County Offaly</a>, which became the burial place of the kings of Osraige. He is the patron saint of Ossory, and some traditions identify him with the Cornish Saint Piran.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ciaran-of-saighir.jpg" alt="Ciarán of Saighir" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas F. Borchert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seir_Kieran_St._Ciaran%27s_Church_SW_2010_09_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The martyrologies, among them the Félire Óengusso, and the medieval genealogies name his father as Lugna, a nobleman of the Dál Birn rulers of Osraige, and his mother as Liadán of the Corcu Loígde. According to the legend, Liadán dreamt before his conception that a star fell into her mouth, and the druids told her that her son's fame would reach the end of the world. Cape Clear Island, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-cork">County Cork</a>, is regarded as his birthplace. His biography is full of obscurities, but it is commonly said that he was already a Christian when he left Ireland to study at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By one account Patrick sent Ciarán ahead of him with a little bell that would not ring until he reached the well where he was to build his monastery. Ciarán settled as a hermit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seir-kieran">Saighir</a> near the Slieve Bloom Mountains, and all four of his Lives describe him dressed in skins with the animals of the forest as his first pupils, an image that recalls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> as forerunner. Disciples gathered, and his mother is said to have joined him with a community of women. Saighir became the chief church of Osraige and a wealthy industrial community, later superseded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canice">Saint Canice</a>'s foundation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe</a>.</p><p>The dating of his life is a long-standing matter of academic disagreement. Plummer, Hogan and Kenny accept a mission before Patrick, which would place his birth near the end of the 4th century, while Baring-Gould, Sharpe, O'Riain and Sperber move his life into the 5th or 6th century. It seems more likely that he was one of the twelve men Patrick consecrated as helpers on his arrival.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His Lives credit him with many miracles: healing a bird taken by a kite, blessing a well whose water tasted of wine or honey, and keeping a fox, a badger and a wolf as monks who cut wood and built huts. He is thought to have died at an advanced age of natural causes. The ruins of his monastery remain, and there are old church ruins and a well on Cape Clear. He is venerated on 5 March in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, a feast he shares with Liadán and his successor Carthach the Elder, and St. Kieran's College, founded in 1782, bears his name. Pádraig Ó Riain considers his identification with Saint Piran groundless.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ciarán of Saigir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>St <b>Ciarán of Saigir</b>, among the earliest Irish saints, was claimed as one of their kin, and a genealogical text on the Corcu Loigde preserves their lineages.</blockquote><a href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a></li><li><blockquote>The monasteries of Saighir, linked with St <b>Ciarán</b>, and later Aghaboe and Kilkenny, where St Canice is honoured, were centres of learning and gave their names to the diocese of Ossory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cléire, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Saighir, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.43,-9.5" aria-label="Cléire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4335 383 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ciarán of Saighir"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4785 653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4785 653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.43,-9.5">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
